31,2,,status,Mio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
32,2,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
33,2,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
34,2,,status,Minako,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
36,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings are sobering. The overflow basin from eight years ago is fine, but the civic hall apron is already patched three times. Also, half the eastern avenue trees died due to poor aftercare.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Misaki,,"My results are equally concerning. Only two firms are tendering for earth-moving, and both warned about clay subsoil and slippage risks, though they are fine with shallow, small-plant work.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Mio,,"I found out that the regional habitat fund only pays for planting and water management, not buildings! Also, the upkeep cost for planted areas is triple that of hard surfaces.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The groundskeeping roll has shrunk from eighteen posts to seven. We are relying on bought-in services for specialist care, though we do have a casual pool for weekend stewarding.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The data confirms my suspicions. Most weekday users are just crossing the site to reach the tram stop on firm ground, and drainage callouts have doubled since the culvert was capped.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are facing a pattern of systemic neglect. If the city cannot even water new trees or maintain a simple paved apron, any high-maintenance greenery will likely perish.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Misaki,,"And with the clay subsoil and slippage risks, any plan requiring deep excavation or heavy earth-moving is a gamble. We should stick to shallow work to avoid delays and cost overruns.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Mio,,"Wait, if planted areas cost triple to maintain and the fund won't pay for buildings, we really have to be careful! We can't afford a plan that's too expensive to keep up.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"Exactly. With only seven staff members left, we cannot possibly manage a complex landscape. We need something that doesn't require constant specialist care or a massive workforce.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Minako,,"And we can't ignore the people. If they are mostly using the park as a thoroughfare to the tram, we shouldn't block those paths or build something that ignores that flow.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Misaki, given the slippage risks and the failure of the civic apron, I suspect a plan with heavy infrastructure or deep foundations would be a disaster. We need something durable and simple.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Misaki,,"I agree. Any design that requires significant earth-moving or heavy structural work will likely drift in schedule and budget. Shallow, lightweight interventions are the only safe bet here.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Mio,,"I'm really worried about the costs! If we pick something with lots of plants, the upkeep will eat our budget alive, especially since the habitat fund won't cover any buildings or paving.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"We also have to consider the volunteers. If we can't afford staff, we need a plan that aligns with the existing volunteer groups' skills, or we're just setting the park up for failure.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Minako,,"I'll add that the drainage issues are critical. If the culvert is already struggling, any plan that doesn't address water management or actually worsens it will just lead to more emergency callouts.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we are in agreement that the ideal plan must be low-maintenance, avoid deep excavation, and address the drainage crisis without relying on a non-existent workforce. Let us see if any candidate actually fits these constraints.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Misaki,,"I'm ready to see the options. If a plan requires massive digging or complex structures, I'll be the first to raise a red flag.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Mio,,I just hope there's a plan that balances being pretty with being affordable! I'll be looking closely at the costs.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Asuka,,I will be looking for a plan that doesn't demand more than our seven remaining staff can handle. Let's see the candidates.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Minako,,"I agree. Let's hope the candidates reflect the reality of the ground and the people, rather than just a designer's fantasy.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,,status,Mio,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,,status,Misaki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
